The percentage-height calculation quirk now stops at a containing block
whose height isn’t auto but isn’t definite, and leaves percentages
inside it no basis. Blink does the same:
BlockNode::UseParentPercentageResolutionBlockSizeForChildren() passes
the parent’s percentage basis through only for an auto height, so a
child of a box with an unresolvable percentage height gets an indefinite
basis. Gecko reaches the same result another way: Its
IsQuirkContainingBlockHeight() lets the search pass a block whose
computed height is unconstrained, so the search goes on to the flex
container, whose auto height leaves the percentage without a basis too.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5
(Playwright’s build), all three give the test’s six cases the same
heights as Ladybird now (720px for the full-height block in their
720px-tall viewport, where test-web’s is 600px).
